<section class="slide">
<span class="sectionno">12</span>
<h2>The Grandfather’s Answer — Central Idea</h2>
<p>The grandfather explains that these differences came about because people had ceased to live by their own labour and had taken to depending on the labour of others.</p>
<p>In the old time, they lived according to God’s law. They had what was their own and did not covet what others had produced.</p>
<div class="two">
<div class="visual">
<div class="flow">
<div class="node">Own labour</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="node">God’s law</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="node">Did not covet others’ produce</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout"><b>📌 Remember:</b> The final explanation connects the size of grain and the condition of the three generations with the change in people’s way of living.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="slide">
<span class="sectionno">13</span>
<h2>Three Generations — Visual Comparison</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Person</th><th>Physical description</th><th>What he says about the grain</th></tr>
<tr><td>First old peasant</td><td>Old and bent, ashy pale, toothless, two crutches; could hardly see and hear.</td><td>Never sowed, reaped or bought such grain; asks the King to question his father.</td></tr>
<tr><td>His father</td><td>One crutch; still able to see; heard better than his son.</td><td>Never sowed, reaped or bought such grain; says his grain was larger and yielded more flour than present-day grain; refers to his father.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grandfather</td><td>Walks without crutches; clear eye, good hearing, distinct speech, sound teeth.</td><td>Recognises the grain as the very same kind and says such corn grew everywhere in his time.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="exam"><b>⭐ Exam Point:</b> The physical condition improves as the story moves from the grandson’s generation back to the grandfather’s generation.</div>
</section>

<section class="slide">
<span class="sectionno">14</span>
<h2>Comprehension Check II</h2>
<div class="question"><b>1.</b> How big was the corn in his father’s time?<div class="answer">The old man says that in his father’s time the grain grew larger and yielded more flour than theirs.</div></div>
<div class="question"><b>2.</b> Read the lines that describe the old man’s grandfather.<div class="answer">He entered walking easily and without crutches; his eye was clear, his hearing good, and he spoke distinctly.</div></div>
<div class="question"><b>3.</b> What was the grandfather’s reply to the King’s question?<div class="answer">Corn like this used to grow everywhere in his time; he lived on it, fed others on it, and sowed, reaped and thrashed it.</div></div>
<div class="question"><b>4.</b> Did people in grandfather’s time buy or sell bread?<div class="answer">No. No one thought of buying or selling bread, and they knew nothing of money.</div></div>
<div class="question"><b>5.</b> Was the land free during his time?<div class="answer">Yes. He said the land was God’s earth, land was free, and no man called it his own.</div></div>
</section>

<section class="slide">
<span class="sectionno">15</span>
<h2>Comprehension Check II — Continued</h2>
<div class="question"><b>6.</b> What were the last two questions of the King?<div class="answer">Why the earth bore such grain then but had ceased to do so now; and why the grandson, son and grandfather had such different physical conditions.</div></div>
<div class="question"><b>7.</b> What reply did the grandfather give?<div class="answer">He said people had ceased to live by their own labour and had begun depending on the labour of others. In the old time they lived according to God’s law and did not covet what others had produced.</div></div>
<div class="callout"><b>🧠 Teaching transition:</b> Now that the story’s central answer is clear, we can revise its key vocabulary and then move to the writing tasks.</div>
