<h2>Final Revision — 15 Key Points</h2>
<ol>
<li>Children found a strange grain in a ravine.</li>
<li>It was shaped like a grain of corn and as large as a hen’s egg.</li>
<li>A traveller bought it for a penny and sold it to the King as a curiosity.</li>
<li>The wise men could not identify it at first.</li>
<li>A hen pecked it and revealed that it was a grain of corn.</li>
<li>The learned men found nothing about it in their books.</li>
<li>They suggested asking the peasants.</li>
<li>The first old peasant had never seen or bought such grain and referred to his father.</li>
<li>The second old man said grain in his time was larger and yielded more flour than present-day grain.</li>
<li>The grandfather recognised the grain.</li>
<li>He said such corn used to grow everywhere in his time.</li>
<li>People grew their own corn and shared when needed.</li>
<li>They knew nothing of money and did not buy or sell bread.</li>
<li>Land was free; labour was the only thing men called their own.</li>
<li>The grandfather connected the change with people depending on others’ labour and coveting what others produced.</li>
</ol>
